.\" /shared/apps/ubuntu/man/man1/rocbudai.1
.TH "ROCBUDAI" "1" "2026-06-02" "rocBudAI" "rocBudAI User Commands"
.SH NAME
rocbudai \- AMD GPU profiling and optimisation AI assistant
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rocbudai
[\fB\-\-continue\fR|\fB\-c\fR]
[\fB\-\-new\fR|\fB\-n\fR]
.PP
.B rocbudai \-h
|
.B rocbudai \-\-help
.PP
\fBrocbudai\fR must run on a Slurm\-allocated compute node; it has
no login\-node mode (allocate the node yourself with \fBsalloc\fR
first \(em see \fBQUICK START\fR).
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBrocBudAI\fR is an AI\-powered performance\-engineering assistant for
AMD GPU clusters, packaged as a Lmod module.
It wires the OpenCode coding TUI to a locally\-hosted qwen3.5:122b
model served by Ollama and is pre\-seeded with knowledge of the AMD
profiling stack (\fBrocprofv3\fR, \fBrocprof\-compute\fR,
\fBrocprof\-sys\fR, \fBrocpd\fR, \fBrocm\-smi\fR).
.PP
.B Privacy / airgap.
rocBudAI runs entirely on the cluster.
Your source code, profiling data, and every chat turn stay on the
allocated compute node \(em they are never sent to any external
service while the \fB\-\-comment=ollama\fR job is running.
The model is served by a local \fBollama\fR daemon, and the kernel
firewall blocks external network egress from BOTH the \fBollama\fR
uid (always, via \fBollama\-egress.service\fR at boot) AND your own
user uid (per\-job, via \fBrocbudai\-user\-egress.service\fR plus a
prolog\-installed nft chain torn down by the epilog).
Audited on demand by \fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check\fR(1).
See also the \fBAIRGAP\fR section below for the full enforcement
mechanism.
.PP
After a seven\-question discovery interview the agent iteratively
builds, profiles, analyses, and optimises the user's code, writing a
running \fBreport.md\fR as it goes.
All work runs on the allocated compute node.
The reference platform is MI300A in SPX mode; the architecture is
GPU\-agnostic.
.PP
The locally\-served model is wrapped with rocbudai\-specific
operating rules at the SYSTEM\-prompt level (5 turn\-invalidating
hard rules: one question per turn, median\-only headline numbers,
no raw \fBEdit\fR on \fBreport.md\fR, profile before iter01 changes,
re\-Read modified files before re\-editing).
The full ruleset with rationale lives in \fBAGENTS.md\fR (loaded as
session context); the SYSTEM overlay enforces the most\-violated
subset at the inference root.
See \fBdeploy/ollama-models/README.md\fR in the rocbudai source tree
for the overlay design, application flow, and rollback procedure.
.SH QUICK START
You need a cluster account and the ability to allocate a full-GPU (SPX)
MI300A compute node (on the reference cluster, partition
\fBPPAC_MI300A_SPX\fR).
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fB# 1. From a login node, allocate an exclusive SPX GPU node.\fR
\fB#    \-p selects the SPX MI300A partition; \-\-comment=ollama tells the\fR
\fB#    cluster's job_submit logic to start the ollama daemon on the\fR
\fB#    node for you.\fR
salloc \-p PPAC_MI300A_SPX \-\-exclusive \-\-comment=ollama \-\-time=02:00:00

\fB# 2. cd into your source tree (rocBudAI works wherever you point\fR
\fB#    it \(em it never copies your code).\fR
cd ~/my-app

\fB# 3. Load the module. The OpenCode TUI auto\-launches with the agent\fR
\fB#    pre-configured for the AMD profiling workflow.\fR
module load rocbudai
.fi
.RE
.PP
There is no login\-node shortcut: \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR and
\fBrocbudai\fR both refuse to run without a Slurm allocation and
print the \fBsalloc\fR command above.
You must allocate the compute node yourself.
.SH SEVEN-QUESTION DISCOVERY INTERVIEW
After the TUI starts the agent asks seven short questions, one at a
time:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ1/7\fR \(em session name (short label used to find this session
again from the \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR picker; see
\fBSESSION MANAGEMENT\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ2/7\fR \(em application type (HIP/C++? PyTorch? Fortran+OpenMP?
MPI?)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ3/7\fR \(em ROCm version (which \fBrocm/<ver>\fR module to load)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ4/7\fR \(em other modules (compilers, MPI, libraries)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ5/7\fR \(em build instructions (your build command, or path to a
build script).
If you do not have source code yet \(em just learning the stack \(em
answer "no source"; the agent will offer to clone an upstream example
from \fBhttps://github.com/amd/HPCTrainingExamples\fR matching your
Q2 answer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ6/7\fR \(em run command (how to launch the binary with a
representative input)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBQ7/7\fR \(em figure of merit (what number defines "faster" for
your application)
.PP
After Q7 the agent echoes the plan, asks for confirmation, then runs
the build \(-> profile \(-> analyse \(-> optimise \(-> re\-profile
loop.
.SH PERMISSION MODES
By default the agent runs in \fBASK\fR mode: every non\-trivial bash
command pauses for a \fBy\fR/\fBEnter\fR approval.
Read\-only commands are pre\-approved (\fBls\fR, \fBcat\fR,
\fBhead\fR, \fBtail\fR, \fBwc\fR, \fBstat\fR, \fBfile\fR,
\fBgrep\fR, \fBrg\fR, \fBfind\fR, \fBtree\fR, the
\fBmodule\fR query subcommands \(em \fBlist\fR / \fBavail\fR /
\fBspider\fR / \fBshow\fR, and the AMD GPU probes
\fBrocm\-smi\fR / \fBrocminfo\fR) so the agent does not nag you
for trivial introspection.
The full allow\-list lives in the managed \fB./opencode.json\fR
(per\-project) and \fB/etc/opencode/opencode.json\fR
(cluster\-wide).
.PP
.B AUTO\-RUN mode
\(em commands run without per\-call approval, but a small set of
dangerous patterns (\fBrm \-rf /\fR, \fBmodule purge\fR, \fBmkfs\fR,
\fBshutdown\fR, \fBreboot\fR) remain hard\-denied at the opencode
layer.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fB# Inside the TUI:\fR
/exit

\fB# Then on the compute node:\fR
export ROCBUDAI_AUTORUN=1
module load rocbudai          \fI# (or: rocbudai \-\-continue)\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
To go back to ASK mode: \fBunset ROCBUDAI_AUTORUN\fR (or
\fBexport ROCBUDAI_AUTORUN=0\fR), then \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR or
\fBrocbudai \-\-continue\fR.
.SH SESSION MANAGEMENT
Exit the TUI with \fB/exit\fR or \fBCtrl\-D\fR.
You remain on the compute node inside your Slurm allocation.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBmodule load rocbudai\fR         \fI# show picker if prior sessions exist; else fresh\fR
\fBrocbudai \-\-continue\fR          \fI# resume the last session in this dir (skip picker)\fR
\fBrocbudai \-\-new\fR               \fI# force a fresh session (skip picker)\fR
\fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions\fR     \fI# interactively delete old sessions (see below)\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Session picker.
A no-flag \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR (or \fBrocbudai\fR with no resume
flag, run on the compute node) shows an interactive numbered menu of
prior sessions associated with the launching cwd, sorted most-recent
first. Pick a number to resume, \fBn\fR to start a new session, or
\fBq\fR to cancel. The picker is bypassed when \fB\-\-continue\fR
or \fB\-\-new\fR is passed (or the corresponding
\fBROCBUDAI_CONTINUE\fR / \fBROCBUDAI_NEW\fR env var is set), and when
stdin/stdout is not a TTY.
.PP
.B Session names.
The first discovery question (\fBQ1/7\fR) asks the user for a short
name. The agent persists it via \fBrocbudai\-name\-session "<name>"\fR
into \fB./.rocbudai\-sessions.json\fR (project\-scoped sidecar). The
picker shows that name next time the user comes back to this dir, so
sessions are findable by intent rather than by cryptic
\fBses_\fR\fIID\fR\fR. Names are best\-effort: if the user skips the
question, the picker falls back to opencode's auto\-generated title.
.PP
.B Filter scope.
When the launching cwd is a normal writable directory, the picker
shows only sessions whose project directory equals it. When the
launching cwd is \fB$HOME\fR or unwritable, the picker shows
sessions across every \fB~/rocbudai\-runs/*/\fR subdir, so the user
gets the same UX regardless of where they reloaded the module from;
picking a session chdirs to its original project dir before launch.
.PP
.B Pruning old sessions.
opencode\(cqs session storage is global \(em deleting files in a project
directory does \fBnot\fR remove the sessions that were opened from
that directory, so the picker can fill up with stale entries (and with
opencode\(cqs auto\-generated titles for sessions that predate the
\fBQ1/7\fR session\-naming feature).
Run \fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions\fR to interactively delete the
unwanted entries:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions\fR             \fI# project scope; pick by index (1,3,5\-7) or "all"\fR
\fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions \-\-dry\-run\fR    \fI# show what would be deleted, without deleting\fR
\fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions \-\-all\-runs\fR   \fI# scope to ~/rocbudai\-runs/*/ (use from $HOME)\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The helper calls \fBopencode session delete\fR for each chosen ID and
removes any matching entry from
\fB./.rocbudai\-sessions.json\fR so the picker\(cqs name cache stays
in sync.
.PP
opencode keys session storage by \fBproject directory\fR (cwd at
launch).
To resume a session you must end up in the directory the session was
created in \(em the picker handles that automatically.
If the original session was auto\-created from \fB$HOME\fR (see
\fBWORKING DIRECTORY\fR below), \fBrocbudai \-\-continue\fR run again
from \fB$HOME\fR will resume the most recent
\fB~/rocbudai\-runs/\fR\fIsubdir\fR automatically.
.SH WORKING DIRECTORY
The directory you launch \fBrocbudai\fR from is treated as the
project: \fBAGENTS.md\fR, \fBopencode.json\fR,
\fB.rocbudai\-runtime.md\fR, and \fBreport.md\fR are written there,
and opencode keys session storage on it.
.PP
If the launching cwd is \fB$HOME\fR or is not writable, the launcher
substitutes a fresh project directory under
\fB~/rocbudai\-runs/\fR\fItimestamp\fR\fB/\fR (so \fB$HOME\fR is not
cluttered with scratch files, and so opencode's per\-session git
snapshot does not interact with the rest of \fB$HOME\fR).
The auto\-created directory persists after logout; the welcome banner
prints its absolute path.
.PP
For \fB\-\-continue\fR / \fB\-\-session\fR the launcher does \fBnot\fR
mint a fresh directory; it resolves to the most recent existing
\fB~/rocbudai\-runs/*/\fR instead, so the session is actually found.
If no prior session directories exist, the launcher refuses with a
clear message rather than dropping you into an empty TUI.
.SH AIRGAP BASELINE
Every \fB\-\-comment=ollama\fR allocation inherits a cluster\-wide
airgap baseline that keeps source code, prompts, model output,
\fBand\fR user\-side commands on the cluster while the job runs:
.IP \(bu 2
Managed \fB/etc/opencode/opencode.json\fR pins
\fB"share": "disabled"\fR and disables 14 cloud providers
(anthropic, openai, etc.). Overrides any project\-local
\fBopencode.json\fR. The modulefile additionally sets
\fBOPENCODE_DISABLE_{AUTOUPDATE,LSP_DOWNLOAD,MODELS_FETCH,EXTERNAL_SKILLS,SHARE}=1\fR
so every known opencode phone\-home call site is suppressed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBollama\-egress.service\fR installs an nft owner\-match egress
block on the \fBollama\fR UID at boot. External traffic from that
UID is REJECTed; only loopback, cluster DNS, and RFC1918 are
permitted.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrocbudai\-user\-egress.service\fR loads an nft skeleton table at
boot, and the Slurm prolog extends it with a per\-job chain that
REJECTs external traffic from the \fBuser's UID\fR for the duration
of the allocation. Loopback, cluster DNS, and RFC1918 remain
reachable. The epilog tears the per\-job chain down.
.IP \(bu 2
The systemd unit for the ollama daemon sets \fBOLLAMA_NOPRUNE=1\fR
and \fBOLLAMA_NO_CLOUD=1\fR (no cloud feature handshake, no
background update probes), and binds the daemon to
\fB127.0.0.1:11435\fR (loopback only).
.PP
Source code, prompts, model output, and user\-side commands all
stay on the cluster while the allocation is running.
.PP
.B What this does NOT cover.
The user\-UID block is per\-job. \fBOutside\fR an allocation, your
UID has full network egress \(em by design, so admin tooling and
pre\-staging dependencies work. If you need to fetch something
external (\fBpip install\fR, \fBgit clone https://...\fR, model
downloads), do it BEFORE allocating with \fB\-\-comment=ollama\fR.
The login node also has unrestricted egress (no allocation, no
per\-job chain). Recursive DNS via the cluster resolver still
returns external IPs, but those IPs are kernel\-blocked, so the
channel is unusable for meaningful exfil. Cluster admins are
trusted.
.PP
.B Verifying the baseline.
After \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR on a compute node, run:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check\fR              \fI# user-visible probes (no sudo)\fR
\fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check \-\-deep\fR       \fI# also inspect nft table contents (sudo)\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The tool reports pass / fail / warn for the four hardening pieces
above and prints clear hints if anything is degraded. Exit code 0
means the airgap is intact.
.SH REPORT FORMAT
The agent writes and updates \fBreport.md\fR in the project directory
across the build \(-> profile \(-> analyse \(-> optimise loop.
Non\-trivial claims are tagged so a reader can distinguish measured
data from interpretation:
.TP
.B [FACT]
Directly measured in the run, stated in source/API headers, or
trivially reproducible from artifacts on disk.
.TP
.B [INFERENCE]
Interpretation consistent with the facts but not proven; confounds
called out explicitly.
.TP
.B [OPINION]
A recommendation or judgement call \(em distinct from inference.
.SH KNOWLEDGE BASE
The cluster admin maintains a curated knowledge base of profiling
slides, papers, and internal notes under
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fB/shareddata/rocbudai/docs/inputs/\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The agent lists this directory at session start, reads the \fB.md\fR
sidecars it judges relevant to your workload type, and cites them in
\fBreport.md\fR.
You can extend the KB yourself by dropping files into the same
directory:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.pdf\fR \(em a \fBsystemd.path\fR watcher on the login node
auto\-converts to a \fB.md\fR sidecar within seconds.
The PDF stays archival; the agent only ever reads the sidecar.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.md\fR / \fB.txt\fR / \fB.html\fR \(em read directly, no
conversion needed.
.PP
New files appear to the agent at the start of the next session.
Files dropped mid\-session are not noticed automatically; ask the
agent in chat to re\-list the directory if needed.
The agent always ignores a file named \fBREADME.md\fR in this
directory \(em do not use that name.
.SS Naming convention
Recommended (the workload\-type prefix lets the agent skip files
unrelated to your run):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fI<workload-type>\fR__\fI<topic>\fR__\fI<source>\fR__\fI<yyyymmdd>\fR.\fI<ext>\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Workload\-type tags: \fBhip-cpp\fR, \fBhip-fortran\fR, \fBpytorch\fR,
\fBtensorflow\fR, \fBjax\fR, \fBmpi\fR, \fBomp\fR, \fBgeneral\fR
(applies to any workload).
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hip-cpp__rocprofv3-quickstart__amd-public__20260301.pdf
pytorch__torch-profiler-recipes__pytorch-org__20260115.md
mpi__rocprof-sys-mpi__amd-blog__20260420.pdf
general__rocm-7.2-release-notes__amd__20260225.md
.fi
.RE
.SS YAML front-matter for hand-authored Markdown
The PDF ingest pipeline auto-prepends YAML front-matter so the agent
can cite the source filename and judge freshness from the extraction
date.
For a Markdown file you author by hand, follow the same convention:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fB\-\-\-\fR
\fBsource:\fR my-internal-writeup
\fBextracted_at:\fR 2026-04-30
\fBworkload_type:\fR pytorch
\fB\-\-\-\fR

# Title

(content...)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without front-matter the agent will still read the file, but it
cannot cite the provenance precisely or down\-weight stale notes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-continue ", " \-c
Resume the most recent opencode session in the project directory
(bypasses the session picker; see \fBSESSION MANAGEMENT\fR).
opencode keys session storage by cwd, so launch from the same
directory as the original session.
If you originally ran from \fB$HOME\fR, run \fB\-\-continue\fR from
\fB$HOME\fR \(em the launcher will resolve to the most recent
\fB~/rocbudai\-runs/*/\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-new ", " \-n
Force a fresh opencode session, bypassing the session picker even
when prior sessions exist for the launching cwd.
See \fBSESSION MANAGEMENT\fR for picker behaviour.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print short usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_AUTORUN
\fB1\fR / \fBtrue\fR / \fByes\fR / \fBon\fR enables AUTO\-RUN mode.
\fB0\fR / \fBfalse\fR / \fBno\fR / \fBoff\fR (or unset) selects ASK
mode (default).
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_CONTINUE
If set to a non\-empty value, equivalent to passing
\fB\-\-continue\fR.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_NEW
If set to a non\-empty value, equivalent to passing \fB\-\-new\fR
(forces a fresh session, bypassing the picker).
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_AGENTS_NOREFRESH
If set, the launcher does not auto\-refresh \fBAGENTS.md\fR from the
canonical template when the template is newer.
Equivalent to \fBchmod 0444 AGENTS.md\fR.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_OPENCODE_NOREFRESH
If set, the launcher does not auto\-refresh \fBopencode.json\fR from
the canonical template when the template is newer.
Equivalent to \fBchmod 0444 opencode.json\fR.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_MODEL
Ollama model name. Defaults to \fBqwen3.5:122b\fR.
Must be in the cluster admin's allow\-list
(\fBROCBUDAI_ALLOWED_MODELS\fR).
End users normally do not override this.
.IP
On this cluster the allow\-list currently contains:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBqwen3.5:122b\fR \(em the default (since 2026\-06\-08).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgpt\-oss:120b\fR \(em opt\-in alternative (the previous default;
OpenAI gpt\-oss MoE).
(\fBexport ROCBUDAI_MODEL=gpt\-oss:120b\fR before \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnemotron\-3\-super:120b\fR \(em opt\-in alternative
(\fBexport ROCBUDAI_MODEL=nemotron\-3\-super:120b\fR before \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR).
.RE
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_NUDGE_AFTER_S
Idle seconds after the assistant turn ends before the auto\-nudge
watcher injects a \fB"continue"\fR prompt.
Defaults to \fB240\fR.
Raise (e.g. \fB360\fR) if you find the watcher firing while you are
still reading or composing your next message; lower (e.g. \fB60\fR)
if you want a tighter loop at the cost of more false positives.
The watcher fires at most once per user message regardless of value.
The nudge is a regular user message (opencode 1.14.28 has no hidden
channel exposed to plugins or to outside posters), so it appears
verbatim in the chat transcript.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_NUDGE_DISABLE
Set to \fB1\fR before \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR to skip launching the
auto\-nudge watcher entirely for this session. No nudges; if the
agent stops mid\-turn you wake it up manually with any prompt.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_NUDGE_TEXT
Override the text the watcher posts (default \fBcontinue\fR).
Rarely needed; useful for A/B testing alternative nudge phrasings.
.TP
.B ROCBUDAI_NUDGE_LOG
Absolute path to which the watcher appends diagnostics
(\fBsession.idle\fR arrivals, nudge fire / skip reasons, POST
outcomes). Empty / unset (default) \(em silent.
Suggested for debugging:
\fBexport ROCBUDAI_NUDGE_LOG=$HOME/.cache/rocbudai/nudge.log\fR
(create the parent directory first).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./AGENTS.md
Agent persona, hard rules, seven\-question script, recovery procedures,
and report format. Auto\-refreshed from the canonical template when
the template is newer.
.TP
.B ./opencode.json
opencode TUI configuration (model, permissions, denied commands).
Auto\-refreshed from the canonical template when newer.
.TP
.B ./.rocbudai\-runtime.md
Per\-session runtime context (node, Slurm job, model, cwd).
Overwritten on every launch; safe to delete.
.TP
.B ./.rocbudai\-sessions.json
Project\-scoped sidecar mapping opencode session IDs to user\-given
names (set via \fBQ1/7\fR / \fBrocbudai\-name\-session\fR).
Read by the \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR session picker and pruned by
\fBrocbudai\-prune\-sessions\fR. Safe to delete \(em sessions then
fall back to opencode\(cqs auto\-generated titles in the picker.
.TP
.B ./report.md
Running report the agent writes during the build\-profile\-analyse\-optimise
loop.
Updated incrementally; not overwritten.
.TP
.B ~/rocbudai\-runs/\fItimestamp\fR/
Auto\-created project directory when \fBrocbudai\fR is launched from
\fB$HOME\fR or an unwritable cwd. Persists after logout.
.TP
.B /shareddata/rocbudai/docs/inputs/
Site knowledge base. Drop \fB.md\fR / \fB.txt\fR / \fB.html\fR / 
\fB.pdf\fR files here for the agent to read at the next session.
See \fBKNOWLEDGE BASE\fR above.
.TP
.B /shared/apps/ubuntu/opt/rocbudai/
Installation root (\fBbin/\fR, \fBshare/\fR, \fBlibexec/\fR).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
TUI exited cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
Environment problem (no Slurm allocation, no controlling TTY,
opencode binary missing or unreadable, ollama daemon unreachable,
model not available, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic interactive session
.RS 4
.nf
\fBsalloc \-p PPAC_MI300A_SPX \-\-exclusive \-\-comment=ollama\fR
\fBcd ~/my-app\fR
\fBmodule load rocbudai\fR
.fi
.RE
.SS Resuming a session
.RS 4
.nf
\fBcd ~/my-app\fR
\fBrocbudai \-\-continue\fR
.fi
.RE
.SS Auto\-run mode
.RS 4
.nf
\fBexport ROCBUDAI_AUTORUN=1\fR
\fBmodule load rocbudai\fR
.fi
.RE
.SS Verify the airgap is in force
.RS 4
.nf
\fBsalloc \-p PPAC_MI300A_SPX \-\-exclusive \-\-comment=ollama \-t 2:00:00\fR
\fBmodule load rocbudai\fR
\fI# in another terminal, ssh to the same allocation and run:\fR
\fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check\fR
.fi
.RE
.SH TERMINAL TIPS
The OpenCode TUI captures the alternate screen and the mouse, which
defeats some terminal-native conveniences (right-click copy, mouse
scroll wheel, native scrollback) you may be used to.
The workarounds below are useful in any terminal but are especially
needed for WSL / Windows Terminal users, where the limitations show
up first.
.SS Paste-friendly output via a second shell
The single most useful workaround \(em \fBdo this first if anything
else here looks fiddly\fR.
The agent writes everything important
(commands, FACT/INFERENCE/OPINION-tagged measurements, profile paths,
file modifications) to \fB./report.md\fR in your project directory
as it works.
Open a second terminal, ssh to the same compute node, and tail the
report:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fI# from another local shell or another VS Code / WSL pane\fR
\fBssh \fIyour-compute-node\fR
\fBcd \fIyour-project-dir\fR
\fBtail \-f \./report.md\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
That window is plain terminal output \(em scrolls natively, copies
natively, no TUI capture.
You read it for paste-friendly numbers and commands; you talk to the
agent in the TUI window.
This works the same on WSL, macOS, native Linux, and any SSH client.
.SS Scrolling inside the TUI
OpenCode supports keyboard-based scrolling inside its message
viewport.
The default keys (verify against your version's
\fBopencode \-\-help\fR or \fB?\fR inside the TUI \(em they have
shifted across releases) are typically Page-Up / Page-Down for
page-level scroll and Ctrl\-U / Ctrl\-D (or
\fB[\fR / \fB]\fR) for half-page scroll.
The mouse wheel may also work depending on your terminal's mouse
forwarding.
Slash commands (typed in the prompt) include \fB/help\fR to list
keybindings for the running version.
.SS Folding the model's "Thinking" panel
qwen3.5:122b emits an internal "thinking" channel that opencode
renders inline.
It is useful when debugging the agent's reasoning but can dominate
the screen during routine work.
Inside the TUI, \fB/thinking\fR toggles whether the thinking panel
is shown for the current session (re-run the slash command to flip
back).
.SS Copy/paste in WSL / Windows Terminal
OpenCode captures the mouse, so a normal click-drag-to-select inside
the TUI is intercepted by opencode and does not enter Windows
Terminal's clipboard.
Two workarounds:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Hold Shift while click-dragging.
Most terminals (including Windows Terminal, GNOME Terminal,
Konsole, iTerm2, kitty, alacritty) treat Shift\-click as
"bypass the application's mouse capture and select natively."
After selecting, your terminal's normal copy gesture
(Windows Terminal: right-click or Ctrl\-Shift\-C) puts the text on
the clipboard.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Use the second-shell + \fBtail \-f report.md\fR pattern above.
Anything you would have wanted to copy out of the TUI is already in
the report file in copy-friendly form.
.PP
For the most polished experience under WSL, run the second-shell
\fBtail \-f\fR in a separate Windows Terminal tab \(em the TUI tab
stays for conversation, the tail tab stays for paste-able output.
.SH MEASUREMENT NOTES
The \fBqwen3.5:122b\fR model that powers rocBudAI is loaded into MI300A
VRAM persistently (\fBOLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=\-1\fR).
On the MI300A SPX nodes the ollama daemon is confined to APUs 0,1,2
(\fBROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1,2\fR plus a CPU cpuset in the ollama unit),
which leaves \fBAPU 3 reserved exclusively for benchmarking\fR.
.PP
This isolation matters because the CPU and GPU of an MI300A APU share the
same HBM. A FOM (figure\-of\-merit) command that runs on the
.I same
APU the model is serving from competes for that APU's compute and memory
bandwidth and can run 10x\(en300x slower \(em pure inference contention,
not a property of the user's code.
A single stress\-test rep on 2026\-05\-13 measured a 250x slowdown on a
1.6 s baseline that completed during an active inference burst.
Running the FOM on the dedicated, model\-free APU 3 removes that
contention so the wall time reflects your code.
.PP
The single\-rep wall\-time number is therefore not a reliable signal on
its own.
You must combine
.B both
of:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA per\-APU quiescence guard\fR \(em wait until \fBrocm\-smi \-u\fR shows
the bench APU (APU 3) below ~5 % busy for several consecutive samples
before launching the command, so each rep starts from the same baseline.
GPUs 0\(en2 are intentionally busy serving the model and are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMultiple reps with median, not mean\fR \(em at least 3 effective reps
(the first rep is dropped as warmup), reported as the median of the
remaining reps; declare an improvement / regression / "within noise"
call only when |delta| > 2 x inter\-rep stddev (see
\fBAGENTS.md\fR Rule 17 / section 5.0).
.PP
The
.B rocbudai\-bench
helper packages both into a single command:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-bench [OPTIONS] \-\- <command> [args...]\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Defaults: 4 reps (drop rep 1, take median of the remaining 3), 30 s
quiescence timeout, 5 % bench\-APU busy threshold, 3 consecutive 1 Hz
quiet samples.
On timeout it prints a WARN line and proceeds.
.PP
Every rep is pinned to the dedicated APU: its GPU is selected via
\fBROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES\fR and the process is bound to that APU's CPU
cores via \fBtaskset\fR, so USM arrays first\-touch into the APU's local
HBM (a single APU is one NUMA region, so there is no \fBnumactl\fR).
The bench APU and its core list are overridable with
\fBROCBUDAI_BENCH_GPU\fR (default 3) and \fBROCBUDAI_BENCH_CPUS\fR
(default 72\-95,168\-191) \(em point these at whichever APU is excluded
from the ollama/agent cpuset if the topology changes.
\fBHSA_XNACK\fR is not forced; \fBexport HSA_XNACK=1\fR yourself if your
code needs managed\-memory page migration.
For MPI FOMs the \fBtaskset\fR sets the APU cpuset and intra\-node rank
binding stays your job (e.g. \fBmpirun \-\-bind\-to core\fR).
On any rep failure it suppresses the summary, prints the last 20 lines
of the failing rep's combined output to stderr, and exits 1.
A single\-rep mode (\fB\-n 1\fR) is available for sanity checks; the
output is tagged \fB[NOT\-FOM]\fR and must not be cited as
\fB[FACT]\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fB# Default: 4 reps with quiescence guard, 3-rep median.\fR
rocbudai\-bench \-\- ./my_app

\fB# 6 reps for a noisy single\-GPU benchmark.\fR
rocbudai\-bench \-n 6 \-\- ./my_app

\fB# Skip quiescence on a node where you fully own the GPU.\fR
rocbudai\-bench \-\-no\-wait \-\- ./isolated_run

\fB# JSON output for embedding the run as evidence in report.md.\fR
rocbudai\-bench \-\-json \-\- ./fom > .bench.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
The agent itself is required to invoke wall\-time FOM commands through
\fBrocbudai\-bench\fR (\fBAGENTS.md\fR Rule 17, "Bench\-helper rule"
sub\-paragraph).
The same helper is available to you on the command line for
hand\-checking the agent's numbers or for running benchmarks outside
the rocBudAI session.
.PP
Multi\-rank or multi\-GPU FOMs use a sibling helper, \fBrocbudai\-submit\fR,
which runs the command on a dedicated multi\-GPU Slurm allocation (one
rank per GPU) and returns the same median/\fB[FACT]\fR summary:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-submit \-\-gpus N \-\- mpirun \-np N ./solver\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
The submit partition comes from \fBROCBUDAI_SUBMIT_PARTITION\fR or \fB\-p\fR,
else the cluster default partition; its GPUs may be smaller partitioned
slices than the bench APU, so treat that number as a separate baseline,
not comparable to the single\-GPU \fBrocbudai\-bench\fR value.
.PP
For the in\-script reference (rationale, exit codes, defaults), run:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBhead \-110 $(command \-v rocbudai\-bench)\fR
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
For a comprehensive preflight (modules, mounts, daemon reachability,
GPU visibility, KFD leak scan, etc.):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-doctor\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
To verify the airgap baseline (managed opencode config, ollama egress
block, daemon hardening) is in force on the current allocation:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check\fR             \fI# user\-visible probes (no sudo)\fR
\fBrocbudai\-airgap\-check \-\-deep\fR      \fI# also inspect nft table contents (sudo)\fR
.fi
.RE
.SS Recovering from a leaked GPU profile (rocbudai-reap-stale)
When a profile run aborts with \fBHSA_STATUS_ERROR_*\fR (any HSA
error code, especially \fBHSA_STATUS_ERROR_MEMORY_APERTURE_VIOLATION\fR),
when \fBrocprofv3\fR logs \fBcaught signal 15\fR or
\fBwill wait for N children to exit\fR, or when a back-to-back retry
fails because "the GPU still looks busy", the on-GPU child of the
crashed run is often still alive in \fBR\fR/\fBRl\fR state holding a
KFD context.
The agent's bash tool returns control immediately, but the leak
persists invisibly in the kernel and pins resources for the next
attempt.
\fBrocbudai\-reap\-stale\fR finds and (optionally) kills these leaked
processes.
.PP
The agent invokes it automatically as part of its profile-recovery
procedure (see \fBAGENTS.md\fR section 5.1).
You can also run it yourself from the compute-node shell at any time:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\fBrocbudai\-reap\-stale\fR              \fI# dry\-run: list leaked KFD children, exit 2 if any\fR
\fBrocbudai\-reap\-stale \-\-reap\fR        \fI# kill them (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10\~s)\fR
\fBrocbudai\-reap\-stale \-\-reap \-\-yes\fR  \fI# same, no interactive y/N prompt\fR
\fBrocbudai\-reap\-stale \-\-help\fR        \fI# all options\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B What it considers a leak.
Any process that
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
appears in \fB/sys/class/kfd/kfd/proc/\fR (the kernel's authoritative
list of processes holding a KFD context),
.IP \(bu 2
belongs to the current \fB$USER\fR (it never touches other users'
processes),
.IP \(bu 2
has been alive at least 5 minutes (configurable via
\fB\-\-age\-min\fR; tunes out short-lived nominal children),
.IP \(bu 2
is not in the built-in allow-list (see below).
.RE
.PP
.B What it will never kill.
The allow-list permanently excludes \fBollama\fR / \fBollama\-real\fR
/ \fBollama\-proxy\fR (the local model server), \fBopencode\fR (the
TUI you are reading this in), \fBpython\fR / \fBpython3\fR (broad —
notebooks, ML scripts, sidecar daemons), \fBjupyter\fR, and the
XFCE/VNC desktop helpers.
The reaper also walks its own ancestor process tree and refuses to
touch any of its own ancestors, so it can never kill the shell that
spawned it.
.PP
.B Exit codes.
\fB0\fR no leaks (or all reaped successfully),
\fB1\fR reap failed (one or more PIDs survived SIGKILL — almost
always means kernel D-state, in which case
\fBscancel $SLURM_JOB_ID\fR followed by re-allocating is the only fix),
\fB2\fR leaks found in dry-run (the agent uses this as the signal to
ask you whether to \fB\-\-reap\fR), \fB3\fR bad usage / environment
problem.
.PP
Common failure modes and fixes:
.IP \(bu 2
\fIno SLURM_JOB_ID\fR \(em you are on a login node; allocate a
compute node first with
\fBsalloc \-p PPAC_MI300A_SPX \-\-exclusive \-\-comment=ollama \-\-time=02:00:00\fR
and re\-run \fBmodule load rocbudai\fR there.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIno controlling TTY\fR \(em the launcher needs an interactive shell;
do not pipe or background it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIollama daemon not reachable\fR \(em re\-allocate with
\fB\-\-comment=ollama\fR; check \fBsystemctl is\-active ollama
ollama\-proxy\fR on the compute node.
.IP \(bu 2
\fImodel '...' not in registry\fR \(em ask an admin to
\fBollama pull\fR the model.
.IP \(bu 2
\fImodule spider openmpi (or hipfort, pytorch, ...) returns 'no
module(s) found'\fR \(em the cluster's Lmod is hierarchical; you
must load \fBrocm/<ver>\fR first to expose ROCm-tier libraries.
The agent does this automatically as part of its build setup; this
only matters when running \fBmodule\fR commands by hand. Recover with:
\fBmodule load rocm/<ver> && module spider openmpi\fR (or
\fBmodule load rocm/<ver> && module avail 2>&1 | grep \-i openmpi\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB$ROCM_PATH (or another module-set variable) reads empty\fR when you
\fBecho\fR it \(em each command runs in its own shell, so a
\fBmodule load\fR done in a previous, separate call is already gone.
This is NOT a stale shell that needs \fBsource\fR-ing. Re-run with the
load chained: \fBmodule load rocm/<ver> && echo $ROCM_PATH\fR (or
\fB&& \fRthe command that needs it).
